<section class="cr-hero"><div class="cr-w cr-hero-g"><div><p class="cr-badge">Home baking, measured</p><h1>Bake it right <span>the first time.</span></h1><p class="cr-lead">Crumbly is an independent home-baking guide. We help you convert cups to grams, swap pan sizes and set the oven correctly, so the recipe you follow turns out the way it should.</p><p class="cr-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the converters</a><a class="cr-ln" href="baking-measurements-guide.html">Why weighing matters &rarr;</a></p></div>
<div class="cr-pol"><figure class="cr-p1"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794670138330-ef33c57279ea?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=600&q=75" alt="stack of cinnamon buns topped with pearl sugar" width="520" height="600" fetchpriority="high"></figure><figure class="cr-p2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794479786386-979d10862db6?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=360&q=75" alt="chocolate chip cookie on a white paper lid" width="360" height="360" fetchpriority="high"></figure><figure class="cr-p3"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794380211146-3bd857126b40?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=360&q=75" alt="flower-shaped apple pastry topped with thyme" width="360" height="360" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="cr-s"><div class="cr-w"><div class="cr-hh"><h2>Three habits of good bakers</h2><p>Small changes that make the biggest difference.</p></div><div class="cr-hab">
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794810788825-c31ea827e7e3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="hand pointing at rolled gingerbread dough" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><span>1</span><h3>Weigh everything</h3><p>A small digital scale is the single most useful baking tool you can own.</p></article>
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793350535476-cd6647f41fbb?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="dinner rolls baking in a pan in the oven" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><span>2</span><h3>Know your oven</h3><p>Ovens run hot or cold. An oven thermometer shows you the real temperature.</p></article>
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794810788817-f88147086998?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="star-shaped cookie dough cutouts on a dark mat" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><span>3</span><h3>Respect the rest</h3><p>Chilled dough holds its shape; rested batter rises more evenly.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="cr-tl" id="tools"><div class="cr-w"><div class="cr-hh cr-hh-w"><h2>Kitchen converters</h2><p>Three quick answers for the moment you&rsquo;re standing at the counter.</p></div><div class="cr-tools">
<div class="cr-tool"><div class="cr-tool-i">&#9878;</div><h3>Cups to Grams</h3><label for="cg-i">Ingredient<select id="cg-i"><option value="125">All-purpose flour</option><option value="120">Bread flour</option><option value="200">Granulated sugar</option><option value="220">Brown sugar (packed)</option><option value="120">Icing sugar</option><option value="227">Butter</option><option value="90">Rolled oats</option><option value="85">Cocoa powder</option><option value="240">Milk</option><option value="340">Honey</option></select></label><label for="cg-c">Cups<input id="cg-c" type="number" min="0" step="0.25" value="1"></label><div class="cr-out" id="cg-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="cr-tool"><div class="cr-tool-i">&#9634;</div><h3>Pan Size Converter</h3><label for="ps-a">Recipe pan<select id="ps-a"><option value="r20">20 cm (8 in) round</option><option value="r23" selected>23 cm (9 in) round</option><option value="s20">20 cm (8 in) square</option><option value="s23">23 cm (9 in) square</option><option value="t33">33 &times; 23 cm (13 &times; 9 in) tray</option><option value="l900">900 g (2 lb) loaf</option></select></label><label for="ps-b">Your pan<select id="ps-b"><option value="r20">20 cm (8 in) round</option><option value="r23">23 cm (9 in) round</option><option value="s20" selected>20 cm (8 in) square</option><option value="s23">23 cm (9 in) square</option><option value="t33">33 &times; 23 cm (13 &times; 9 in) tray</option><option value="l900">900 g (2 lb) loaf</option></select></label><div class="cr-out" id="ps-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="cr-tool"><div class="cr-tool-i">&#9832;</div><h3>Oven Temperature</h3><label for="ot-v">Temperature in the recipe<input id="ot-v" type="number" step="5" value="180"></label><label for="ot-u">Recipe is in<select id="ot-u"><option value="c" selected>&deg;C (conventional)</option><option value="cf">&deg;C (fan)</option><option value="f">&deg;F</option><option value="g">Gas mark</option></select></label><div class="cr-out" id="ot-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="cr-s"><div class="cr-w cr-chk"><div><div class="cr-hh cr-hh-l"><h2>Is it done yet?</h2><p>How to tell, bake by bake.</p></div><dl class="cr-done"><div><dt>Cakes</dt><dd>Springs back when pressed; a skewer comes out clean.</dd></div><div><dt>Cookies</dt><dd>Edges set and golden; centres still look slightly soft.</dd></div><div><dt>Bread</dt><dd>Deep golden crust; sounds hollow when tapped underneath.</dd></div><div><dt>Brownies</dt><dd>A skewer comes out with moist crumbs, not wet batter.</dd></div><div><dt>Pastry</dt><dd>Golden all over, including the base; layers look dry and crisp.</dd></div></dl></div><div class="cr-band"><p class="cr-band-q">&ldquo;Measure twice, bake once.&rdquo;</p><p>The oldest kitchen advice is still the best. A recipe written in grams and baked at the right temperature works the same in every kitchen.</p><a class="btn cr-btn-l" href="pan-size-guide.html">Read the pan guide</a></div></div></section>

<section class="cr-s cr-butter"><div class="cr-w"><div class="cr-hh"><h2>From the guides</h2></div><div class="cr-gds"><a href="baking-measurements-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794480902624-3b4aa94e5944?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="slice of blueberry cake with cream frosting" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 1</span><h3>The Baking Measurements Guide</h3><p>Grams, cups, spoons and the conversions that matter most.</p></div></a><a href="pan-size-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793125411995-24ca8b5a332e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="walnut-shaped cookies with caramel filling" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 2</span><h3>The Pan Size Guide</h3><p>Swapping pans, scaling batter and adjusting the bake time.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="cr-s"><div class="cr-w cr-fq"><div class="cr-hh cr-hh-l"><h2>Baking questions</h2><p>The ones we hear every week.</p></div><div class="cr-faq"><details open><summary>Why weigh instead of using cups?</summary><p>A cup of flour can weigh anywhere from about 120 g to 160 g depending on how it is scooped. Weighing removes that guesswork, which is why baked goods turn out the same every time.</p></details><details><summary>How do I measure flour by cup if I have no scale?</summary><p>Stir the flour, spoon it lightly into the cup until it overflows, then level it with a knife. Do not scoop straight from the bag or tap the cup, which packs in extra flour.</p></details><details><summary>Can I bake a recipe in a different pan?</summary><p>Yes, if you scale the batter to the new pan&rsquo;s area and adjust the time. A deeper layer needs longer and sometimes a slightly lower temperature.</p></details><details><summary>What is the difference between fan and conventional ovens?</summary><p>A fan oven circulates hot air, so it cooks faster. As a rule of thumb, lower the temperature by about 20&deg;C (25&deg;F) from a conventional recipe.</p></details><details><summary>Why did my cookies spread too much?</summary><p>Common causes are butter that was too warm, too little flour (often from scooping inaccurately), a hot baking tray or too much sugar. Chilling the dough for 30 minutes helps.</p></details><details><summary>How do I know when a cake is done?</summary><p>The top springs back when pressed lightly, the edges pull away from the pan and a skewer inserted in the centre comes out clean or with a few moist crumbs.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="cr-nl"><div class="cr-w cr-nl-in"><div><h2>The Crumbly Note</h2><p>One baking fix and one conversion trick, once a month.</p></div><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@crumbly.com?subject=The%20Crumbly%20Note&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

